Article 7 Catering service providers shall take the following measures to prevent food waste:
(1) establish and improve management systems for food procurement, storage, and processing, strengthen professional training for service personnel, and incorporate the values of cherishing food and opposing waste into the training content;
(2) proactively remind consumers to prevent food waste, post or place anti-food-waste signs in conspicuous positions, or have service personnel give prompts and explanations, guiding consumers to order food in appropriate quantities according to their needs;
(3) improve the quality of food supply, prepare food in accordance with standards and norms, reasonably determine the quantity and portion size, and offer choices in different sizes such as small portions;
(4) where group meal services are provided, integrate the concept of preventing food waste into menu design, and reasonably arrange dishes and staple foods according to the number of diners;
(5) where buffet services are provided, proactively inform consumers of the consumption rules and requirements for preventing food waste, provide tableware in different sizes, and remind consumers to take food in appropriate amounts.
Catering service providers shall not induce or mislead consumers into ordering excessive amounts of food.
Catering service providers may enrich menu information by indicating food portions, sizes, recommended number of consumers, etc., provide ordering prompts to consumers, and provide serving spoons, serving chopsticks, and doggy bag services according to consumers' needs.
Catering service providers may give rewards to consumers who participate in the "Clean Plate Campaign"; they may also charge consumers who cause obvious waste a corresponding fee for the disposal of kitchen waste, and the charging standard shall be clearly indicated.
Catering service providers may use information technology to analyze dining needs, and through measures such as building central kitchens and distribution centers, scientifically manage food procurement, transport, storage, and processing.
